Here's a C++ function that converts markdown formatted text to HTML formatted text:

```cpp
#include <string>
#include <regex>

std::string markdownToHtml(const std::string& markdown) {
    // Replace markdown tags with HTML tags
    std::string html = markdown;
    std::regex pattern(R"((\*?)\*?>)");
    std::regex_replace(html, pattern, "<p>$1</p>");
    return html;
}
```

This function uses a regular expression to replace markdown tags with HTML tags. The regular expression matches the markdown tags (`<*>`) and replaces them with HTML tags (`<p>`). The `std::regex_replace` function is used to perform the replacement.

Here's an example usage of the function:

```cpp
#include <iostream>

int main() {
    std::string markdown = "This is a *markdown* text";
    std::string html = markdownToHtml(markdown);
    std::cout << html << std::endl;
    return 0;
}
```

This will output:

```
<p>This is a <p>markdown<p> text</p>
```

Note that this function only handles basic markdown tags and does not support all markdown features. For more advanced features, you may need to use a markdown parser.

